)⌋ for d sufficiently large.\nEquiangular lines with a fixed angle\n\nIntroduction  \nEquiangular lines with a fixed angle  \nBounded Degree Lemma  \nEigenvalue Multiplicity Lemma\n\nDefinition 10 (Nα(d))  \nLet Nα(d) be the max size of equiangular lines in ℝ^d with angle α.\n\nDefinition 11 (Spectral radius order k(λ))  \nGiven a λ > 0, we define k = k(λ) = inf{|G| : λ1(G) = λ}.\n\nTheorem 12 (Jiang, Tidor, Yao, Zhang, Zhao, 2019)  \nFix α ∈ (0,1). λ = (1−α)/(2α) and k = k(λ). Then  \n1. If k < ∞, then Nα(d) = ⌊k(d−1)/(k−1)⌋ for d sufficiently large.  \n2. If k = ∞, then Nα(d) = d + o(d) as d → ∞.\n\nNote: If α−1 is odd, λ = (1−α)/(2α) = (α−1+1)/2 ∈ N ⇒ k(λ) = λ + 1.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGraphs and Combinatorics           (2023) 39:25 (https://oro.open.ac.uk/87574/1/373_2023_Article_2619.pdf)\nciteturn1search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Turán Problems for k-Geodetic Digraphs\nJames Tuite1 · Grahame Erskine1\n· Nika Salia2,3 ... Erd˝os conjectured in 1975 that the largest possible size of a graph with order n and girth ... 2 + o(1))3/2n3/2 [12]; this conjecture remains open.\nGraphs and Combinatorics           (2023) 39:25 \nhttps://doi.org/10.1007/s00373-023-02619-x\nORIGINAL PAPER\nTurán Problems for k-Geodetic Digraphs\nJames Tuite1 · Grahame Erskine1\n· Nika Salia2,3\nReceived: 28 March 2022 / Revised: 27 January 2023 / Accepted: 31 January 2023\n© The Author(s) 2023\nAbstract\nA digraph G is k-geodetic if for any pair of (not necessarily distinct) vertices u, v ∈\nV (G) there is at most one walk of length ≤k from u to v in G. In this paper, we\ndetermine the largest possible size of a k-geodetic digraph with a given order. We\nthen consider the more difﬁcult problem of the largest size of a strongly-connected\nk-geodetic digraph with a given order, solving this problem for k = 2 and giving a\nconstruction which we conjecture to be extremal for larger k. We close with some\nresults on generalised Turán problems for the number of directed cycles and paths in\nk-geodetic digraphs.\nKeywords Digraph · Turán Problem · Extremal · k-geodetic · Strong-connectivity\nMathematics Subject Classiﬁcation 05C35 · 05C20\n1 Introduction\nTurán problems are a fundamental part of extremal combinatorics. Such a problem\ntypically asks for the largest possible size of a graph G with a family F of forbidden\nsubgraphs. When F consists of small cycles, this is equivalent to the girth problem.\nErd˝os conjectured in 1975 that the largest possible size of a graph with order n and girth\nat least 5 is given by ( 1\n2 + o(1))3/2n3/2 [12]; this conjecture remains open. Lazebnik\nB Grahame Erskine\ngrahame.erskine@open.ac.uk\nJames Tuite\njames.t.tuite@open.ac.uk\nNika Salia\nsalianika@gmail.com\n1\nOpen University, Walton Hall, Milton Keynes, UK\n2\nInstitute for Basic Science, Daejeon, South Korea\n3\nAlfréd Rényi Institute of Mathematics, Budapest, Hungary\n0123456789().: V,-vol \n123--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSchildkraut: Equiangular lines and large multiplicity of fixed second eigenvalue (https://yufeizhao.com/blog/2023/03/06/schildkraut-eigval/)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.7 years ago; Crawled: 2.0 years ago; In my previous work on equiangular lines (with Zilin Jiang, Jonathan Tidor, Yuan Yao, and Shengtong Zhang) (previously reported on this blog and MIT News), we solved the following problem: ... Theorem (Jiang–Tidor–Yao–Zhang–Zhao). ... The quantitative bound is the bottleneck being able to improve the “sufficiently large dimension” part of the equiangular lines result. ... Here is a question relevant to the equiangular lines problem with a fixed angle (appearing as Question 6.4 in our equiangular lines paper):\nFurthermore, we define $k(\\lambda) = \\infty$ if no such graph $G$ exists.\n\nTheorem (Jiang–Tidor–Yao–Zhang–Zhao). For $\\alpha \\in (0,1)$. Let $\\lambda = (1-\\alpha)/(2\\alpha)$ and let $k(\\lambda)$ be its spectral radius order. Then the maximum number $N_\\alpha(d)$ of equiangular lines in $\\mathbb{R}^d$ with common angle $\\arccos \\alpha$ satisfies:\n(a) If $k(\\lambda) < \\infty$, then $N_\\alpha(d) = \\lfloor k(d-1) / (k-1) \\rfloor$ for all sufficiently large $d$;\n(b) If $k(\\lambda) = \\infty$, then $N_\\alpha(d) = d + o(d)$ as $d \\to \\infty$.\n\nIn particular, this result determines the first order growth rate of $N_\\alpha(d)$ for all $\\alpha$. Even better, when the spectral radius order is finite, our result determines the answer exactly in all sufficiently large dimensions. (There is a tantalizing open question on how high the dimension needs to be.) However, when the spectral radius order is infinite, our result gives only an asymptotic $d + o(d)$. Can we hope for a more precise answer?\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTuran problems for $k$-geodetic digraphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.04957)\nciteturn1academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.7 years ago; Authors: James Tuite, Grahame Erskine, Nika Salia ... We then consider the more difficult problem of the largest size of a strongly-connected $k$-geodetic digraph with given order, solving this problem for $k = 2$ and giving a construction which we conjecture to be extremal for larger $k$. We close with some results on generalised Tur\\'{a}n problems for the number of directed cycles and paths in $k$-geodetic digraphs.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAUSTRALASIAN JOURNAL OF COMBINATORICS (https://ajc.maths.uq.edu.au/pdf/80/ajc_v80_p419.pdf)\nciteturn1search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; vertex arboricity, the following analogue of Brooks’ Theorem is a well-known result. ... Conjecture 1.3 (Equitable Vertex Arboricity Conjecture [27]). ... 2-degenerate graphs, 3-degenerate claw-free graphs with maximum degree at least 4,\n---------------------------------